Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer qualifies as a Superior Proposal?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: Section 9.7 Superior Proposal Evaluation.
Notwithstanding any contrary provisions, during the Pre-Closing Phase, the Board may determine that an incoming Acquisition Proposal qualifies as a Superior Proposal only if:
(i) the Board, after reviewing the complete financial details provided and consulting with an independent financial advisor, verifies that the financial metrics meet the expected benchmarks;
(ii) the Board must concurrently consult with external legal counsel to ensure that the Proposal complies with all applicable regulatory, contractual, and fiduciary obligations;
(iii) if negotiations lead to significant modifications in non-financial terms, a supplementary review process shall be initiated to assess the impact of these changes on the overall merit of the Proposal;
(iv) even in the event that the financial analysis is favorable, the presence of any legal advisories or concerns raised by shareholders regarding operational or market risks requires a full reassessment.
This multi-tier review process mandates that both quantitative financial analysis and qualitative non-financial factors are considered, making it clear that the financial point of view is not the sole determinant for classifying an offer as a Superior Proposal.
(Page 119-121)
