Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is "financial point of view" the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: Section 9.7 – Assessment of Superior Offers. The Company Board is required to conduct a comprehensive review of any Superior Offer. In doing so, the Board shall evaluate not only the quantitative financial metrics, including revenue forecasts and cost synergies, but also qualitative factors such as customer sentiment, market positioning, and sustainability impacts. In addition, the Board must consider its fiduciary duties under applicable state law and must obtain independent legal counsel and strategic advisory opinions. Section 9.7(a) further provides that if negotiations with a third party lead to substantial changes in non-financial terms, the Board must reinitiate its review process to ensure that all material factors, both financial and non-financial, are integrated before confirming the superiority of an offer. Section 9.8 states that any amendment altering the risk profile of the assets involved triggers a re-evaluation of the proposal.\n
